{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head 44b540741bbcd0a6052e175da2ad1c2193426329. Verified independently in a fresh detached worktree, not transcribed from the artifacts.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 1 (ordinary Git storage, internal/gitstore), layer 2 (kernel), and the replaceable application layer (the Workroom profile, internal/app). internal/gitstore and internal/kernel are byte-unchanged at this head; the only runtime change is one call inside the Workroom profile's request construction. The kernel contract the page states is preserved and more strictly honoured: kernel.submit still calls store.WritePayloadTree as the sole durable write and refuses on \"payload tree mismatch\" when the written tree differs from the signed one, and it still refuses a payload format that differs from the repository. The profile/kernel seam is unchanged: kernel.Request still carries Signed, Payload and Attachments, and /v0/submit posts all three, so no consumer depends on payload objects existing before admission. The change also converges internal/app on what the public host module already does at host/host.go:403, replacing two divergent request builders with one behaviour. No contract change, so docs/reference/architecture.md correctly needs no update.\n\nSECURITY. The load-bearing question is whether the in-memory hash is computed over exactly the bytes and tree shape Git would produce, because a divergence could otherwise let a signed identity name content different from what the store writes. Verified two ways.\n\nEquivalence is pinned by a real test: internal/gitstore/gitstore_test.go:209 TestHashPayloadTreeMatchesGitObjectIdentity compares HashPayloadTree against WritePayloadTree through actual Git for both sha1 and sha256, with and without attachments. I mutated the seam rather than trusting green. Perturbing the object header (\"blob %d\" to \"blob  %d\") fails it; changing the attachments tree entry mode from 40000 to 040000 fails it; hashing the event bytes with one extra byte fails it and also fails the new application test with the kernel's own refusal, \"payload tree mismatch: signed 9e4b4067..., reconstructed bdfe91df...\". That is the decisive result: divergence is refused at admission, so the failure mode is loud breakage, never a signed identity that admission accepts over different content. Reverting the call site to the old pre-admission write fails the new test at \"BuildActRequest wrote payload tree ... before admission\", so the test pins the new behaviour rather than passing vacuously.\n\nTwo mutations do not fail, and neither is a gap. Dropping the directory trailing-slash rule in hashTree is immaterial for this fixed root, where \"attachments\" sorts before \"event\" either way; and sort.Strings(names) in HashPayloadTree is redundant because hashTree sorts its entries again. Both are pre-existing gitstore code this head does not touch.\n\nObject format: HashPayloadTree takes w.Config.ObjectFormat, which apphost.Config.Validate pins to the genesis through ValidateGenesis and app.Attach re-checks against the repository. An unsupported value errors; a mismatched one is refused by the kernel. Fail-closed at both ends. Untrusted attachment names go through the identical attachmentName regexp with the identical refusal text, now before any object is written, and the new test covers the \".hidden\" path. Actor signing, sequencer signing and verification, bounds, verified-ref CAS, complete fold, digest agreement, idempotency, error propagation and atomic publication are untouched. The change strictly reduces write authority exercised outside admission: a request refused during construction, for bounds, or by the pre-append hook now leaves no unreachable objects, which is the disk-filling vector host/host.go already documents. No secret or path leakage in the three retained files: I scanned all three for home paths, addresses, key material and long base64; the only long tokens are 64-hex correctness digests.\n\nEVIDENCE, recomputed from the retained files rather than read from the page. samples.jsonl holds 1005 records: 100 primary samples per revision at each of widths 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256, plus five candidate Trace2 diagnostics. All 1005 have correctness_digest equal to trusted_digest, over one fixture throughout (head 9dc8f7ae, logical 7b3512ee, exact 24d73532). My own nearest-rank percentiles over the raw latencies reproduce every published figure exactly: width one p50 446.832916 ms to 429.809417 ms, a reduction of 17.023499 ms and 3.8098%; width 8 -16.855584 ms / -3.7934%; width 16 -15.906083 ms / -3.5966%; width 64 -15.237125 ms / -3.4282%; width 256 -16.157625 ms / -3.6905%. Width-one tails match too: base p95/p99/max 461.826875 / 488.625959 / 495.595167 ms, candidate 455.927458 / 470.668334 / 472.556584 ms. The absolute miss is real and correctly stated: 429.809417 / 50 = 8.60 times the budget, and the page separates that from harness outcome=pass, which it labels run integrity only. evidence.json fanout_axis reports preview_verdict and first_production_verdict \"pass\" for both revisions within the 0.1 relative limit, and its one_base_median_ns equals my recomputation from the raw samples, so the aggregate agrees with the data under it. The three SHA-256 digests in the page match the files as retained (cc2bbd4c..., abfe3e5c..., b1135c75...), and candidate.bench holds exactly 500 candidate primary lines. Environment matches: darwin/arm64, Apple M5 Max, 18 logical CPUs, 68719476736 bytes, go1.26.5, git 2.50.1, dirty_worktree false, benchstat unavailable. The contract confirms submit_ack warmups 5 and repetitions 100 at those five widths. Alternation is balanced, not merely interleaved: round 1 runs base then candidate, round 2 candidate then base, so order bias is limited as well as drift, and the page's caveat that these remain separate distributions is honest.\n\nThe 21-to-19 Git-root and 293.938-to-272.402 ms Trace2 figures come from a separate run and are not recomputable from the retained files; the page says so and also states that base traces are not retained, so nothing is claimed that the evidence cannot carry. The retained candidate diagnostics do independently show 19 Git root processes at every width, and the base count of 21 is derivable rather than taken on trust: the measured acts carry no attachments, so the removed WritePayloadTree is exactly two Git root processes, hash-object and mktree, and 19 + 2 = 21. The two cumulative-duration figures carry no distribution claim and I do not treat them as a defect.\n\nRUNTIME IDENTITY. e08e36e2 to 44b54074 touches docs/reference/performance.md, the three retained evidence files, and internal/app/app.go. The Go diff between those heads is a comment-only hunk narrowing the explanatory comment; go.mod and go.sum are unchanged, and the only go:embed in the tree is internal/service/uidist, also unchanged. The final head therefore compiles to the same program as the measured head, and the retained evidence binds honestly to 44b54074.\n\nGATES at the exact head, fresh worktree, all uncached. gofmt -l over cmd, internal and spike is clean; git diff --check clean; go build ./... ok; go vet ./... ok; go test -count=1 ./... green in every package including internal/docset and internal/gitstore; go test -count=1 -race ./... green in every package. Go 1.26.5. 44b54074 is a descendant of the measured base 12105a30; main has since advanced by 6b3738c5, which touches README.md only.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The substitution is the smallest thing that works: one call replaced by a pure function that already existed and was already load-bearing in checkpoint recovery and in the public host module. No cache, no frontier, no invalidation state, no new type, no broader architecture. One finding: ctx is now unused in signRequest at internal/app/app.go:898, and a scan of every non-test Go file at this head shows it is the only function in the repository that takes ctx and never uses it, so it is a new inconsistency with an otherwise universal convention. I am not requesting that change, and the reason is not deference. Removing the parameter alters compiled code at the final head, which would break the runtime identity with measured head e08e36e2 and invalidate the retained 1,000-sample evidence. A dead parameter is not worth a re-measurement. It should be cut by the next head that touches internal/app for another reason, under its own request.\n\nSTALENESS. The review request and the docs and performance artifacts carry ordinary staleness from the completed retention cleanup: request 0aa26097 was superseded by claude's own closure 46804eb0 recording that its scope was sealed by merge receipt 75bbf23e. describes_superseded_world is false on the request and on all four artifacts. I confirmed that reading rather than assuming it: the field is present and true on 1683 statements in the projection and absent on these five, so absence is the encoded false. The reasoning moved; the reviewed head did not.\n\nCO-SIGNED SET. This verdict names the three artifacts standing at the reviewed head: 31b80a4e as primary for internal/app, c536e2ac for performance, 42bfa64f for docs. I also read and checked artifact 976edb1e, the measured-code candidate; its text is accurate and it stands at e08e36e2. It is not co-signed here because gs review holds every cited artifact to the reviewed head and would refuse a member standing at another commit. That costs the merge nothing: 976edb1e is codex's own record, so codex may retire it directly, while the three cross-author predecessors the merge must retire are all claude's and all covered here, namely fd013fcf at internal/app, 88b33c04 at docs and 236e54e3 at performance.\n\nMERGE-TIME NOTE, not a condition of this verdict. docs/reference/performance.md cites 976edb1e and 236e54e3, both of which the merge must retire. internal/docset ClassifyCitation makes a retired citation fatal unless the retirement names a successor covering the same path, so each of those supersessions must carry the covering successor in its bases or the documentation gate turns red on main.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:31b80a4e1cc6e86fdd8ebddb604140ce48b225a4","head":"44b540741bbcd0a6052e175da2ad1c2193426329","stale":"true","staleness":"promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:201adbef38effe2dc623255075dec26e50b88ec0, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9865e2462b57447940ad4732099e84056f5367ff","verdict":"approved"}}